# Code Standards — autoparts

What correct code looks like in this repo. Process rules — when to ask, what to verify, what must change together — live in `AGENTS.md`. Each rule appears in exactly one file.

## 1. Stack

- **Next.js 16** (App Router) — see `AGENTS.md` §1 before using an unfamiliar API
- **React 19**
- **TypeScript**, strict mode
- **Tailwind CSS**
- **Base UI** (`@base-ui/react`) via shadcn, style `base-nova` — this project does **not** use Radix
- **Lucide React** icons
- **Zod** validation
- **Prisma 7** with `@prisma/adapter-pg` against Supabase — no other ORM

## 2. Conventions

- **Never use `any`.** Define proper interfaces or types. Avoid unnecessary `as` assertions.
- Components are arrow functions.
- Server Components by default. `'use client'` only for interactivity (`useState`, `useEffect`) or browser APIs, and keep client components small — never put server-executable logic inside one.
- **Every async operation is wrapped in try-catch and has an explicit return type.** Validate input with Zod at the boundary.
- One responsibility per function. Split a function that exceeds ~60 lines.
- No magic numbers — name constants (`const MAX_ITEMS = 100`).
- DRY and SOLID.

**Naming**

- Components: PascalCase — `UserButton.tsx`
- App Router folders and files: kebab-case — `user-profile/page.tsx`
- Utils and hooks: camelCase — `useLocalStorage.ts`

## 3. Where code lives

| Path | Contents |
|---|---|
| `app/` | Routes and layouts |
| `app/admin/_components/` | Admin-only components, including the shared print layer at `app/admin/_components/print/` |
| `components/shared/` | Reusable business components — the main component home (~106 files) |
| `components/ui/` | shadcn primitives, deliberately small (button, dialog, sheet, alert-dialog) |
| `components/{admin,analytics,liff,seo,storefront}/` | Domain-specific components |
| `lib/` | Server utilities, DB client, domain services |
| `lib/generated/prisma/` | Generated by `prisma generate` — never edit |
| `hooks/`, `types/` | Custom hooks, shared type definitions |

## 4. CSS & UI

- Tailwind for all styling; avoid inline styles.
- Mobile-first responsive design.

## 5. Security (OWASP Top 10 + modern web)

### Input & validation

- **Always validate with Zod on the server.** Never trust client-supplied data.
- **Never use raw SQL** or string interpolation in queries — Prisma's parameterized API only.
- Never render user input as raw HTML. `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` is banned unless the content is sanitized with DOMPurify or equivalent.
- Validate file uploads server-side: MIME type, size, and extension, before storing.

### Authentication & authorization

- **Protect every admin route** with a NextAuth session check — never rely on a client-side redirect.
- Use `requirePermission()` at the top of every protected page and every mutating Server Action.
- **Never expose passwords, tokens, or secrets** in API responses or client-side code.
- Hash passwords with `bcryptjs`, cost factor ≥ 12. Never store plaintext.
- Session tokens stay `httpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Lax` — do not override the NextAuth defaults.

### Environment & secrets

- **Never hardcode secrets**, API keys, or credentials — `.env.local` only.
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_`-prefixed variables reach the browser. Never put a secret there.
- Verify a required variable exists in `.env.example` before relying on it.
- `.env` and `.env.local` stay in `.gitignore`.

### HTTP & headers

Security headers in `next.config.ts`:

- `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`
- `X-Frame-Options: DENY`
- `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin`
- `Permissions-Policy` disabling unused browser features
- `Content-Security-Policy` on all pages that do not require third-party inline scripts

### API & Server Actions

- **Every Server Action verifies the session before any write.**
- Return generic error messages to the client — never a stack trace or raw DB error.
- Log errors server-side with request context; never log passwords, tokens, or PII.
- Rate-limit sensitive endpoints (login, OTP, file upload) via middleware or an upstream layer.
- Server Actions are CSRF-protected by default — do not disable it.
- Custom `/api/*` routes restrict `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to known domains — never `*` in production.

### Dependencies

- Run `npm audit` regularly; fix high and critical findings before deploying.
- Pin versions — no `*` or overly broad ranges.

## 6. Prisma & schema

- Use **`prisma db push`**, not `migrate dev` — the Supabase transaction pooler has no shadow database.
- **All `DateTime` fields use `@db.Timestamptz(3)`** unless the user approves a narrower PostgreSQL type for that specific field. Never a bare `DateTime` or `timestamp without time zone`; storage must preserve the instant semantics used by `lib/th-date.ts`.
- Price and cost decimals: `@db.Decimal(10,2)`.
- Quantity decimals: `@db.Decimal(12,4)`, to support fractional units.
- All `StockCard` quantities are stored in **base unit** (scale = 1). Convert to display units in the UI layer only.
- Any field used in `where`, `orderBy`, or as a frequently joined foreign key needs an `@@index`.

## 7. Stock & moving average cost

- Every stock-affecting transaction calls `writeStockCard()` inside `db.$transaction()`.
- **Never update `Product.stock` or `Product.avgCost` directly** — always through `writeStockCard()`.
- `avgCost` changes only when stock comes in (`qtyIn > 0`). Outgoing transactions never change it.
- Neutral stock-in sources (`RETURN_IN`, `CLAIM_RETURN_IN`, `CLAIM_RECV_IN`) use the current running `avgCost` as `priceIn` so they do not distort the average.
- Document numbers are `{PREFIX}{YYYYMMDD}{4-digit}` and always come from `generateDocNo()` — never hardcoded.
- `StockCard` queries must use the `[productId, docDate, sorder]` index — no full scans.

## 8. Document cancellation

- **Stock recalculation is mandatory.** When a stock-affecting document is cancelled, delete its `StockCard` rows, then call `recalculateStockCard(productId, tx)` for every affected product. That re-runs MAVG from scratch and updates `qtyBalance`, `priceBalance`, `Product.stock`, and `Product.avgCost`. Never cancel without it.
- **AR/AP clearing is mandatory.** Cancelling any document carrying an outstanding balance (credit sale, receipt, credit-debt CN) immediately reverses the related balance. Never leave AR/AP dangling.
- **Reference-chain check is mandatory.** Before cancelling or editing a source document, verify no ACTIVE downstream document references it. If any do, reject and list their document numbers. Reverse order:
  - Purchase → cancel purchase returns first
  - Sale → cancel CN and receipts first
  - Receipt → may be cancelled directly, but must reverse AR

## 9. Audit log

- `AuditLog` is **append-only** — never edited or deleted from the UI or any normal application flow.
- **Every admin mutation writes an entry**: create, update, cancel, permission change, password change, export, and special operations.
- Each entry captures at minimum: actor, action, entityType, entityId or entityRef when available, and before/after or equivalent metadata.
- Wiring the audit entry is part of the definition of done for any new page or Server Action that changes persistent data.

## 10. Notifications — bell and Telegram always together

- **Every notification goes to both the in-app bell and Telegram in a single call.** Never wire only one half.
- The sole exception is `NotificationType.GENERAL` (free-form internal catch-all). Every other event flows through `createNotification()` so the bell row and Telegram message dispatch together.

Adding a new admin alert:

1. Add a `NotificationType` enum value in `prisma/schema.prisma`.
2. Define a helper in `lib/notifications.ts` (e.g. `notifyShopeeOrderImported()`) that calls `createNotification()` with that type.
3. `shouldSendTelegramForNotification()` in `lib/telegram.ts` is **opt-out, not opt-in** — it returns true for everything except `GENERAL`. Do not narrow it without explicit approval.
4. Wrap the call site in try-catch (or a `safeNotify*()` helper) so a notification failure never breaks the business flow.

Derived-data bell sections — UI panels querying domain tables like `Customer` directly rather than `Notification` — must also dispatch a `createNotification()` event where the underlying data is created, so admins watching only Telegram still get the alert. No bell-only side channels.

Telegram uses `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` and `TELEGRAM_CHAT_IDS`. If unset, Telegram is silently skipped and the bell still works.

## 11. Dates and time

- **All displayed dates use the Gregorian calendar.** Use locale `"th-TH-u-ca-gregory"`, never `"th-TH"` — the latter renders Buddhist Era years and causes confusion.
- **Date-only business fields** — `saleDate`, `purchaseDate`, `receiptDate`, `claimDate`, `expenseDate`, `adjustDate`, `advanceDate`, `paymentDate`, and lot MFG/EXP dates — are date-only, not UTC timestamps:
  - never `new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10)` for `<input type="date">` defaults
  - never serialize with `date.toISOString().slice(0, 10)`
  - never parse `YYYY-MM-DD` with bare `new Date(value)` or `new Date(\`${value}T00:00:00\`)`
  - use `lib/th-date.ts`: `getThailandDateKey()` for defaults, `formatDateOnlyForInput()` for edit-form serialization, `parseDateOnlyToDate()` / `parseDateOnlyToStartOfDay()` / `parseDateOnlyToEndOfDay()` for server and query handling, `formatDateThai()` / `formatDateTimeThai()` for display
- **Timestamp fields stay real instants** — `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `sentAt`, `resolvedAt`, `returnedAt` and other event times persist UTC-safe and display through `formatDateTimeThai()`.

The `th-date-lint` subagent checks compliance with this section.

## 12. Admin page standards

- Every admin page that queries the database exports `export const dynamic = "force-dynamic"`.
- **Every route segment under `app/admin/(protected)/` has a `loading.tsx`** — list, detail, new, and edit alike. Next.js uses Suspense-based per-segment loading; without it, navigation shows no indicator.
- Multi-line-item forms submit items as JSON: `formData.set("items", JSON.stringify(items))`.
- Errors display in user-friendly Thai — never a stack trace or raw DB error.
- Success messages include the document number when a document is created.

### Dropdowns — `SearchableSelect` is mandatory

Use `<SearchableSelect>` from `@/components/shared/SearchableSelect` instead of a native `<select>` whenever:

- the dropdown pulls from a master table (products, customers, suppliers, categories, brands, expense codes, document numbers), **or**
- the option count exceeds 10, static or dynamic

Native `<select>` is acceptable only for fixed enums of ≤ 10 items (sale type, VAT type, CN type, adjustment type) and the unit selector inside a line-item form.

```tsx
import SearchableSelect, { type SelectOption } from "@/components/shared/SearchableSelect";

<SearchableSelect
  options={items.map((i): SelectOption => ({ id: i.id, label: i.name, sublabel: i.code ?? undefined }))}
  value={selectedId}
  onChange={setSelectedId}
  placeholder="Please select..."
/>
// With native FormData, add a paired hidden input:
<input type="hidden" name="fieldName" value={selectedId} />
```

### Date range filter — required on every transaction list

Required on `purchases`, `purchase-returns`, `sales`, `credit-notes`, `receipts`, `expenses`, `adjustments`, `warranties`, `delivery`.

- **Default**: empty — show all records until a date is entered.
- **Params**: `?from=YYYY-MM-DD&to=YYYY-MM-DD`
- **UI**: two date pickers; filtering applies as soon as either is set.
- **Query**: add `where: { [dateField]: { gte: fromDate, lte: endOfDay(toDate) } }` only when `from` or `to` has a value. Both empty means no date filter at all.
- **`dateField` by type**: purchases / purchase-returns → `purchaseDate`; sales → `saleDate`; credit-notes → `cnDate`; receipts → `receiptDate`; expenses → `expenseDate`; adjustments → `adjustDate`; warranties → `startDate`; delivery queue → `saleDate`

## 13. Performance

Targets: page load under 3 seconds (TTFB + render) on admin and storefront; LCP < 2.5s, INP < 200ms, CLS < 0.1. Speed matters, but **data integrity is never traded for it.**

### Queries

- **No N+1.** Use nested `include` / `select` instead of querying inside a loop.
- Always `select` only the columns actually used.
- List pages cap results with `take` — 100 by default, never more than 200 — or paginate.
- `Promise.all()` for independent parallel queries.
- Order operations inside `$transaction` by dependency; never hold one open longer than necessary.
- Before committing a new query on a large table, assess how it behaves as data grows (`StockCard` at 10,000+ rows). If a page is likely to exceed 3 seconds, say so and propose pagination, an index, or caching before implementing.

### Rendering & caching

- Maximise Server Components; minimise JavaScript sent to the client.
- Frequently changing data: `export const dynamic = "force-dynamic"` (mandatory for admin DB pages, §12).
- Stable storefront data: static generation or `revalidate`.
- `revalidatePath()` after mutations, scoped to affected pages — never the whole cache.
- `useTransition` around Server Action calls so the UI does not block.

### Assets & bundle

- Always `next/image` with explicit `width`, `height`, and a `sizes` prop matching the real layout. Never a bare `<img>`.
- Fonts via `next/font` (Kanit, Sarabun) to preload and prevent FOUT.
- Named imports only — never import a whole library for one function.
- Third-party scripts via `next/script` with `strategy="afterInteractive"` or `"lazyOnload"`.

## 14. SEO & storefront

### Logic preservation

- SEO changes are **purely additive**. Do not touch Server Actions, DB queries, stock logic, pricing, or document flows.
- Allowed surfaces: `metadata` exports, JSON-LD components, `alt` props, page descriptions, `lib/seo.ts`, `components/seo/`, and static content files.
- If an SEO task requires touching a file containing business logic, stop and ask (`AGENTS.md` §6).

### Disruption & performance

- Minimise visible disruption. Do not move elements, change layouts, or alter information hierarchy unless the task requires it.
- Inject SEO content below existing content or into `<head>` — never replace or reorder visible content.
- New visible elements must not add clutter; confirm before adding one.
- Never increase CLS — avoid inserting elements that push content down on load.
- JSON-LD renders server-side. Never inject structured data with `useEffect`.

### Phased rollout

Follow the SEO rollout plan in `PLAN.md` strictly. Do not skip phases or bundle work from several phases into one deployment. Each phase is deployed, verified, and monitored for 3–5 days before the next begins. If a phase causes a regression — traffic drop, CWV degradation, crawl errors — pause the next phase and investigate.

## 15. Absolute prohibitions

- Never edit `lib/generated/prisma/*` — auto-generated. This holds for shell redirects too; the write hook only guards `Write`/`Edit`.
- Never import `pg.Pool` directly — use `PrismaPg({ connectionString, ... })`.
- Never use `any`.
- Never commit `.env`, `.env.local`, or any file containing secrets.
- Never write raw delete / drop / truncate without an explicit instruction.

### Thai text must never be corrupted — iron rule

Every file created or modified preserves Thai characters exactly.

- Save source files as **UTF-8 without BOM**. Never Windows-1252, TIS-620, or any non-UTF-8 encoding.
- CSV and Excel exports for Thai users **must** carry a UTF-8 BOM (`\uFEFF`) so Excel opens them correctly.
- Write Thai string literals as real Thai characters — never `\uXXXX` escapes as a substitute for readable text.
- **Run `npm run check:mojibake` after writing any file containing Thai text.** Garbled output (`àµÒ`) means the encoding is wrong and must be fixed before proceeding. Do not substitute visual inspection for the check.
- Prefer surgical edits on Thai-bearing files (`AGENTS.md` §2) — a full rewrite re-emits every Thai character and is the main way mojibake enters this repo.
